# Trivy ignore list — PRE-EXISTING, documented findings only (README
# "CI & security" reports these separately and honestly). The Docker+Trivy job
# fails on any other fixable HIGH/CRITICAL finding.
#
# Pre-scan method (2026-10-26, Trivy 0.69.3, no local Docker daemon available
# in the build environment): the final image is `python:3.11-slim` + apt
# packages + the uv-managed /opt/venv from uv.lock. The base image was scanned
# daemonlessly (trivy pulls the image itself) and the venv contents were
# scanned as the exact locked requirements export — the venv layer has 0
# fixable HIGH/CRITICAL findings (pip-audit agrees). What remains are two
# packages that ship INSIDE python:3.11-slim's system site-packages; they are
# not dependencies of this application (the app runs from /opt/venv and never
# imports them) and they cannot be fixed through uv.lock because they are not
# in it. They are ignored here explicitly — not silently — and should be
# revisited when the base image tag moves (or removed in favour of upgrading
# them inside the Dockerfile if they ever become app-reachable).
#
# Note: the Docker build could not be executed in the build environment (no
# Docker daemon). If the first CI run of the docker-scan job reports further
# fixable HIGH/CRITICAL findings (e.g. from the apt layer), they must be
# reported and either fixed or added here with the same documentation standard
# — never dropped from the gate.

# jaraco.context 5.3.0 (fixed 6.1.0) — path traversal via malicious tar
# archives. Preinstalled build tooling in python:3.11-slim
# (/usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages); not imported by the app and not
# present in /opt/venv.
CVE-2026-23949

# wheel 0.45.1 (fixed 0.46.2) — privilege escalation / code execution via a
# malicious wheel file. Same story: preinstalled in the base image's system
# site-packages; only relevant when *building* wheels from untrusted input,
# which this image never does at runtime.
CVE-2026-24049
